


:: valetia_session_6_1
<<set $valetia_session_6 = true>>
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>
<<set $valetia_trust += 1>>

The summons arrives in Veletia's handwriting, looping and extravagant, letters dressed in flourishes that would embarrass a peacock. One line on cream parchment, sealed with a wax rose.

<em>Darling. Today we build the queen, at the Bloom Room, at dawn. Bring nothing, I provide everything.</em>

I turn the note over in my fingers and feel the old knot tighten under my ribs. Five sessions have passed in Veletia's care. Five mornings of silk against skin I barely recognize anymore, of lace settling over flesh softer than it has any right to be, of a slow education in garments I never wore and never imagined wearing, brassieres, panties, chemises, the private architecture of a body Veletia keeps narrating like she's unveiling a gallery.

I survived it. I even found pieces of understanding I wouldn't admit out loud, the way a properly fitted brassiere does for a tender chest what silence does for pain, not pleasure exactly, just the absence of constant irritation. The way silk against changed skin feels less like giving up and more like a second skin growing in. Small mercies, kept to myself.

But undergarments stay hidden. A secret between my body and the fabric holding it. What Veletia wants today is something else.

Today we build outward. Today the world gets to look.

The Bloom Room is bright with morning light when I arrive. Veletia has thrown open the tall windows over the Royal Garden, and gold sun pours across the marble floor. The room smells of lavender and cedar, her signature, worked into every fabric that passes through her hands.

She stands at the center beside a dressmaker's form, a padded mannequin shaped, I notice with an uneasy little jolt, to match my current measurements. Modest bust. Narrowed waist. Hips curving outward with the first suggestion of the body I'm becoming. Seeing my own shape rendered in wood and cotton, stripped of face and name, feels like looking at a ghost of myself, pinned in place and waiting to be dressed.

Four garments hang on the form, arranged with Veletia's usual precision. Four layers laid out like pieces of an equation I'm about to wear.

<<speech "veletia">>
Behold, darling. The anatomy of a queen's dress. From skin outward, chemise, corset, bodice, gown. Four layers. Four purposes. Four steps between nakedness and sovereignty.
<</speech>>
<br>

She sweeps a hand across them like a general surveying a battlefield. Her fiery curls, pinned high with golden clasps shaped like tiny scissors, catch the light and blaze.

<<speech "veletia">>
Sessions one through five taught you what sits against the skin. The invisible foundation. Today we build the part everyone sees. Today I dress you the way the court will see you dressed, and you'll understand why a queen's gown isn't a garment. It's a fortress.
<</speech>>
<br>

I look at the four layers. The chemise I recognize, plain linen, softer than my training tunics, meant to protect skin from the rougher fabric above it. The corset is different from Aldric's combat version, more structured, decorative boning, a shape built for sculpture rather than support. The bodice is stiffened silk, richly embroidered, made to sit over the corset and frame the torso. The gown is midnight blue velvet, heavy and glossy, a neckline that dips just enough and sleeves that flare into a cascade at the wrist.

A queen's armor. Silk instead of steel. Still armor.

<<speech "veletia">>
We begin with the chemise. Strip, darling. Down to what's underneath.
<</speech>>
<br>

I hesitate only a moment. Five sessions have burned most of the shame out of me. Veletia has seen every inch of my changing body, measured and assessed it with the flat expertise of a craftsman checking her materials. She's watched the breast buds stop being buds and become small, undeniable swells. She's run a measuring cord around my narrowing waist and called it promising. She's noted, with something close to professional pride, that my hips are finally offering what she calls "the essential geometry."

I undress. The morning air touches skin smoother than it used to be, finer grained, the body hair thinning as the potions do their slow work. The brassiere from Session 3 holds the modest breasts in place, silk cups just beginning to fill with tissue that's warm and present. The panties sit on hips wider than they were a month ago, the elastic tracing a pelvis reshaping itself by the millimeter.

I stand in the Bloom Room in my underclothes and let Veletia look.

She circles once, then twice, her green eyes shrewd and merciless, tracking every place where the old body is losing ground to the new one.

<<speech "veletia">>
Good, the foundation holds. Now we build.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Continue to the chemise|valetia_session_6_2]]

:: valetia_session_6_2

The chemise goes on first, like it always does, but this one isn't the plain sleeping garment from earlier sessions. This is a layering chemise, cut longer, past the knees, a wider neckline that sits below the collarbone and sleeves ending just above the elbow. The linen is fine woven, nearly see-through in the morning light, soft as Maera's voice when she thinks I'm asleep and whispers things she isn't supposed to say out loud.

Veletia guides it over my head. The linen settles against my skin like an old friend's hand, warm right away, draping over the brassiere and panties beneath. The hem brushes my calves. The neckline sits wide across my collarbones, baring skin paler and smoother than I remember, the training tan fading as the transformation changes even how my skin takes the sun.

<<speech "veletia">>
The chemise does three things. First, it protects your skin from the rougher fabric above it. The corset's boning, the bodice's stiffening, the gown's weight, all of it would chafe without this barrier. Second, it soaks up sweat. A queen does sweat, whatever the court likes to pretend. The chemise keeps the outer layers dry. Third, and nobody says this part out loud, it gives you a layer between your body and the world. When the gown comes off, the chemise stays. You're never bare. Just intimate.
<</speech>>
<br>

She adjusts the neckline, tucking a fold behind my shoulder where the fabric caught on the brassiere strap. Her fingers are quick, businesslike, a woman who talks through cloth the way Aldric talks through steel.

<<speech "veletia">>
Notice how it hangs differently than it would have three moons ago. The bust, small as it is, breaks the drape now. The fabric doesn't fall straight from shoulder to hip anymore. It catches at the chest, releases, catches again at the hip. That's the feminine drape. Every garment you wear from now on will move like this. The body shapes the cloth. The cloth tells the truth about the body.
<</speech>>
<br>

I look down. She's right. On the prince's flat chest this would have hung like a sack. Now it catches and gathers and flows, clinging to the small swells of my breasts, easing across a softening stomach, catching again at the hips before it falls to the knees. The fabric tells the truth, and the truth is curved where it used to be straight.

<<speech "veletia">>
Feel the weight of it. Raise your arms.
<</speech>>
<br>

I raise my arms. The chemise lifts with them, the hem climbing my calves, fabric pulling gently across my chest. Linen sliding over the brassiere's cups, cloth against cloth against breast, sends a small strange warmth traveling down and settling behind my sternum.

<<speech "veletia">>
Good, the chemise moves with you. When you raise your arms to take a crown, to wave to your people, to draw a weapon if it comes to that, the chemise follows. It's your constant companion. Learn its weight. Learn where it pulls and where it gives, because every layer above it will make those feelings louder.
<</speech>>
<br>

She steps back, studying me the way a sculptor studies the first pass of clay.

<<speech "veletia">>
Now, the corset.
<</speech>>
<br>

The word drops into my stomach like a stone into still water.

[[The corset lesson|valetia_session_6_3]]

:: valetia_session_6_3
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

I've worn a corset before, the training version Aldric and Veletia built together for combat. That corset was functional, almost military, meant to compress and support through the violence of swordwork. It held me together. It didn't shape me.

This corset shapes.

Veletia lifts it off the form like a holy relic. Ivory satin, vertical channels of whalebone, steel busk clasps up the front, silk lacing at the back, curving inward at the waist and outward at bust and hip with mathematical precision. Along the upper edge, stitched so fine it's nearly invisible, runs a pattern of interlocking vines. Her mark of craft.

<<speech "veletia">>
The court corset isn't the combat corset. They're cousins, not twins. The combat corset holds you steady. The court corset holds you beautiful. The combat version compresses the bust so it doesn't bounce. The court version lifts the bust so it's presented. The combat version binds the waist for stability. The court version narrows it for silhouette. You follow?
<</speech>>
<br>

I follow the way a soldier follows the difference between a drill and a real battle, in my head, not yet in my body.

<<speech "veletia">>
Arms up, darling.
<</speech>>
<br>

She wraps the corset around my midsection, over the chemise. The satin is cool against the linen, smooth and slick, already heavier than the training version. She fastens the busk clasps at the front, and the corset closes around my torso like a pair of firm hands.

Then she starts lacing.

There's no way to describe a court corset tightening without sounding dramatic, and I'm in real danger of sounding both dramatic and slightly hysterical. The laces draw the back panels together, the whalebone stiffens, and the corset starts imposing its will on my body with the polite inevitability of a queen giving an order.

My waist compresses. Not painfully, Veletia is too skilled for that, but firmly, a constriction that makes me sharply aware of every rib, every breath, every inch of torso being reshaped by architecture instead of alchemy for once. The bottom edge presses against my widening hips and holds. The top edge, the important one, pushes up beneath my breasts, lifting them, gathering them, pushing them forward with an insistence the training corset never had.

I gasp. Not from pain. From the sudden, dizzy awareness that the small swells on my chest, which I've spent weeks learning to ignore, have just been turned by the corset's engineering into something visible, prominent, and put on display. The chemise, pushed up by the corset's bust line, gathers into a scoop across my upper chest that frames the new cleavage, shallow, barely a shadow between the modest curves, but there. Impossible to miss.

<<speech "veletia">>
Breathe into the belly, not the chest, Aldric's already taught you this. The corset won't let you take a full chest breath. That's on purpose. A queen breathes from her core. It reads as calm. It reads as control. A woman gasping for air in a corset is a woman nobody bothered to teach.
<</speech>>
<br>

I breathe the way Aldric taught me, low, into the belly, the diaphragm dropping instead of the ribs expanding. The corset allows it. The breath comes easier, and the constriction settles from alarm into discomfort into something almost tolerable.

<<speech "veletia">>
Now look at yourself.
<</speech>>
<br>

She turns me toward the full-length mirror on the Bloom Room's east wall, framed in gilded roses, her altar, and in it I see myself the way the corset wants me seen.

The waist is narrower. Not dramatically, the transformation had already started that work, the corset just emphasizes it, but visibly, a sculpted dip between ribs and hips that suggests more curve than the body actually has. Above the waist, the bust is lifted and gathered, pressed together and up into a shape that promises more than exists. Below, the hips, already widening week by week, flare outward, and the corset's lower edge frames the flare like a border around a painting.

The figure in the mirror isn't the prince. It isn't the queen yet either. But it's unmistakably shaped like a woman.

<<speech "veletia">>
This is what the court will see. Not your body, your silhouette. The corset builds the silhouette. It's the canvas everything else gets painted on. Without it the gown just hangs. With it the gown lives.
<</speech>>
<br>

I stare at the reflection. Wider hips than the person who walked in. A narrower waist. A bust, modest still, lifted into something you'd notice instead of something to hide. Hair falling past the shoulders in waves that frame a face going softer by the day.

I don't recognize this person. But I'm starting to understand them.

<<speech "veletia">>
How does it feel?
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Admit a reluctant fascination|valetia_session_6_4a]] 

[[Express discomfort but determination|valetia_session_6_4b]]

[[Ask to continue without dwelling on it|valetia_session_6_4]]

:: valetia_session_6_4a
<<set $femininity_style += 2>>
<<set $valetia_trust += 1>>
<<set $confusion += 1>>

The word feels small, a thimble trying to hold the sea, but it's the truest one I have.
<br>
<<speech "mc">>
It's strange. It looks deliberate, like somebody designed this shape on purpose, like the body isn't just changing at random, it's being sculpted toward something specific.
<</speech>>
<br>

Veletia's smile is slow and warm and entirely too knowing.

<<speech "veletia">>
Because it is, darling. The potions don't just change you, they refine you, and the corset doesn't just compress, it reveals. You're seeing, maybe for the first time, the shape your body was always going to be made for. The shape sleeping inside the prince since the day the Echofire chose this bloodline.
<</speech>>
<br>

She rests her hands on my corseted waist, light, warm, the touch of someone handling something precious.

<<speech "veletia">>
The shape of a queen.
<</speech>>
<br>

I look at the mirror for a long moment. The queen looks back.

<<speech "mc">>
Let's continue.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<goto "valetia_session_6_4">>

:: valetia_session_6_4b
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>
<<set $defiance += 1>>

The honesty of it surprises me too.
<br>
<<speech "mc">>
It feels like a cage. A beautiful cage. Every breath measured. Every expansion checked. The body Aldric's teaching me to trust, the body I'm learning to fight in, is being held down.
<</speech>>
<br>

Veletia doesn't flinch. She's heard worse from me and taken it the same way Aldric takes my failures in the training yard, not with agreement, but with patience.

<<speech "veletia">>
A cage keeps things in. A corset keeps things up. There's a difference, darling, and you'll learn it. The corset isn't holding your strength back, it's directing your presence. When you stand before the court in this, every eye will see a body lifted by its own architecture, not a body fighting its bonds. That's a kind of power the rapier never taught you.
<</speech>>
<br>

I meet her eyes in the mirror. Green and fierce and certain. Giving up would be easier, and somewhere between Day One's humiliation in the Iron Garden and today's reflection in the Bloom Room, I promised myself I'd stop choosing easy.

<<speech "mc">>
Keep going.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<goto "valetia_session_6_4">>

:: valetia_session_6_4
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

The bodice is the third layer, and with it the garment stops being structure and starts being art.

Veletia lifts it off the form, a fitted piece of embroidered silk in deep emerald green, stiffened, boned at the seams, cut to sit over the corset like a second skin. The front is flat and smooth, the neckline lower than the corset's own edge, showing the gathered chemise and the faintest shadow of cleavage above it. It laces closed at the back with silk ribbons. Gold thread traces leaves along the hem and neckline, her signature stitched into every inch.

<<speech "veletia">>
The bodice is the face of the torso. It's what the world sees first. It announces color, rank, mood, allegiance. A red bodice whispers danger. A white one claims innocence. Black demands silence. Green speaks of growth, of becoming. I thought it fit.
<</speech>>
<br>

She guides it over my head and arms. It settles onto the corset with the precision of measurements taken and taken again, Veletia's endless attention to the body underneath. Stiffened silk presses against satin, the two layers bonding, the corset's structure held and contained by the bodice's shell, the waist even more defined, the bust even more framed.

The neckline sits just below my collarbones, showing the hollow of my throat and the upper curve of the chemise beneath. The sleeves fit to the elbow, then flare into a trumpet of green silk that falls to the wrist. When I move my arms, the flares sway like wings.

<<speech "veletia">>
The bodice talks through fit. Too loose and you're a child playing dress up. Too tight and you're a sausage in silk, which is a look, I'll admit, just not a queenly one. The perfect fit says something different.
<</speech>>
<br>

She tugs the back lacing. The bodice compresses over the corset, and together the layers grip my torso, firm and elegant at once, architecture shaking hands with anatomy.

<<speech "veletia">>
The perfect fit says I belong in this, I was made for this, and everyone in the room believes it.
<</speech>>
<br>

I look at the mirror again. Three layers now. Chemise, corset, bodice. The figure reflected is, there's no other word for it, beautiful. Not the raw beauty of a face caught in candlelight, the crafted, deliberate beauty of something designed. The emerald bodice frames the torso like a painting's border. The waist is impossibly narrow, impossible because the body underneath hasn't actually changed that much, the illusion is the corset's gift, its trick of compression and redirection. The bust, modest as ever, is presented with authority, not flaunted, not hidden, acknowledged. Here are the queen's breasts, the bodice says. They exist. They're real. They're part of the architecture.

My hair falls against the emerald silk, dark against green, the contrast sharp enough that Veletia makes a small, satisfied sound in her throat, the sound of an artist watching her vision come together.

<<speech "veletia">>
Three layers, and already you're someone the court would watch. But watching isn't enough. They need to be unable to look away. For that, the gown.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[The gown|valetia_session_6_5]]

:: valetia_session_6_5
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

The gown is the weight.

I expected that much. I've seen court gowns on my mother, on Lady Miralyn, on every noblewoman at every formal function in the Great Hall. They move in those things with an effortless grace that hides the physical reality of ten pounds of fabric hanging from shoulder to floor. I assumed, with the special arrogance of someone who's never carried weight they didn't choose, that a gown couldn't be harder than armor.

I was wrong.

Veletia unhooks it from the form and holds it open. Midnight blue velvet falls from her arms in a cascade so rich it seems to swallow the light, deep and dense, the nap shifting between blue and black as the folds move. The neckline is a wide scoop that will sit at the collarbones, matching the bodice underneath. The sleeves run long, fitted to the elbow, then explode into trailing points that will brush the floor when my arms hang at my sides. The skirt, and here's the real challenge, is full, gathered at the waist, falling in heavy pleats with enough fabric to fill a small room.

<<speech "veletia">>
The gown is the statement. The chemise is the secret. The corset is the structure. The bodice is the face. The gown is the voice. It speaks before you open your mouth. It enters a room before you cross the threshold. It commands attention, respect, and, worn right, a little fear.
<</speech>>
<br>

She lifts it over my head. The fabric falls like a curtain, heavy and warm, velvet sliding over bodice and corset and chemise in one long, total friction. It settles on my shoulders and I feel the weight, not crushing, just constant. Present in a way that's hard to ignore. The pleats drop around my legs and puddle at my feet, and suddenly I'm enclosed in a column of fabric that cuts my stride down to small, deliberate steps.

My arms drop to my sides. The pointed sleeves hang past my hands. The neckline sits exactly where Veletia planned it, at the collarbones, showing the throat and upper chest, the chemise's gathered neckline visible within, the faint suggestion of the body underneath carried through four layers of fabric and meaning.

<<speech "veletia">>
Don't move yet. Stand still and feel the weight. Let the gown show you where it wants to go.
<</speech>>
<br>

I stand. The velvet presses against me from shoulder to floor, warm, heavy, alive with the particular gravity of formal clothing. Every layer beneath it adds its part, the corset's grip, the bodice's frame, the chemise's whisper. Together they hold me in a shape that's more than the sum of its parts, a shape that isn't just female but regal, not just dressed but assembled.

I breathe. The corset governs the breath. The bodice passes it to the gown. The gown moves, barely, a gentle rise and fall of velvet at the bust, a small shift at the waist, and in that movement the layers talk to each other. They move together. They breathe together. They feel, in a way that's not quite awe and not quite fear, less like a covering and more like an extension of the body.

<<speech "veletia">>
Now, the mirror.
<</speech>>
<br>

I turn, slowly, because the skirt's weight demands slow turns, velvet swinging with a momentum that keeps going after my body stops, and face the full-length mirror.

The reflection stops me cold.

Four layers. Chemise, corset, bodice, gown. The person in the mirror is a woman. Not a prince in women's clothing. Not a boy playing dress up in his mother's wardrobe. A woman. Midnight blue velvet drapes from shoulders narrower than memory, across a bust the corset has lifted into prominence, down a waist the bodice has cinched narrow, over hips the potions have widened into the start of curves, and falls to the floor in pleats that whisper against the marble with every shift of weight.

My hair, longer, darker, finer than the prince's cropped cut, lies against the velvet's nap in waves that catch the light. My face in the mirror is softer than it was even a week ago, the jaw rounding, the cheekbones rising, lips fuller, eyes bigger in a face losing its hard angles and finding something else. Something that, in the deep blue of the velvet and the gold of the morning sun, is hard to call anything but beautiful.

I don't know what to do with beautiful. I was handsome once, the careless way young princes are handsome without ever thinking about their faces. Beautiful is a different country. Beautiful comes with expectations and consequences and a weight heavier than any velvet.

<<speech "veletia">>
There she is. My masterwork. Still in progress, but oh, darling, what progress.
<</speech>>
<br>

She stands behind me, hands on my velvet-covered shoulders, her face appearing beside mine in the mirror. The contrast between us is vivid, her fiery curls and dramatic features against my dark waves and uncertain stillness, her flamboyance against my quiet.

<<speech "veletia">>
What do you see?
<</speech>>
<br>

I look at the mirror. The woman in it looks back. She wears four layers of civilization over a body being rewritten by magic, and in those layers she isn't less than the warrior who trains in the Iron Garden, she's more, more visible, more present, harder to dismiss. A sword can be sheathed. A gown can't be hidden.

<<speech "mc">>
I see someone who can't go back.
<</speech>>
<br>

Veletia squeezes my shoulders. Her reflection smiles, not the theatrical grin, not the showman's smirk, a real, warm, proud smile.

<<speech "veletia">>
No, darling. You're right, and forward is so much more interesting.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Continue the session|valetia_session_6_6]]

:: valetia_session_6_6
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

The rest of Session 6 is instruction in the mechanics of being dressed.

That sounds simple. It isn't. The prince has dressed himself since he was six, tunic, trousers, boots, cloak. The process took three minutes and needed no thought, no help, no coordination harder than lacing a jerkin. A queen's dress is a military operation that needs planning, precision, and ideally reinforcements.

<<speech "veletia">>
A queen doesn't dress herself. Not because she can't, you'll learn to manage it in an emergency, but because the garments are built for assistance. The corset laces at the back. So does the bodice. The gown hooks at points you can't reach. That's not weakness, that's architecture. A fortress isn't weak just because it needs a garrison.
<</speech>>
<br>

She takes me apart, gown off, bodice off, corset loosened, then walks me through the whole dressing process from the start, narrating each step like she's reading from a military manual.

Step one, the chemise. Over the head, adjusted at the shoulders, smoothed at the hips. The hem has to fall even, no bunching, no twisting. The neckline has to sit level across the collarbones. Thirty seconds allotted.

Step two, the corset. Wrapped around the midsection over the chemise. Busk clasps engaged from the front, bottom first, then up, like closing a door from the floor. Lacing starts at the waist, the narrowest point, and works outward, down to the hips, up to the bust. Tightening comes gradual and even, with pauses to breathe. The wearer breathes in, the dresser pulls. The wearer breathes out, the dresser secures. A rhythm as intimate as a heartbeat. Three minutes allotted.

Step three, the bodice. Over the head and arms, settling onto the corset. The front gets smoothed down, any wrinkle here shows through the gown above it. The back lacing is pulled snug, not tight, the corset does the shaping, the bodice just displays it. Sleeves adjusted. Neckline checked. Two minutes allotted.

Step four, the gown. The hardest step. The dresser holds it open, Veletia demonstrates, arms spread wide, velvet hanging like a curtain, and the wearer steps in from behind, guiding her arms into the sleeves as the dresser lifts the fabric over her shoulders. The skirt settles with a gentle shake of the hips, sending the pleats falling into their natural hang. The dresser hooks the back at three points, waist, mid back, between the shoulder blades. Two minutes allotted.

Total, about eight minutes from chemise to finished queen. With practice, Veletia promises, it drops to five.

I'm standing in my underclothes after the third full teardown, sweat damping the brassiere, the morning's glamour thoroughly gone.
<br>
<<speech "mc">>
With practice. How much practice?
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "veletia">>
Your mother can be fully dressed in four minutes, but she's been doing this for twenty three years. I expect you competent at eight minutes within a fortnight. Five within a month. Four minutes, before the coronation.
<</speech>>
<br>

The coronation. The word lands in my chest like a dropped stone. The crown. The gown. The court watching. The kingdom watching. Me, in four layers of silk and velvet and whalebone, standing in front of all of them in a body still becoming, still unfinished, still caught somewhere between prince and queen.

<<speech "veletia">>
Again, darling. From the chemise.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Dress again, focusing on speed|valetia_session_6_7]]

:: valetia_session_6_7
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

We dress and undress seven times. By the fourth try I can engage the corset's busk clasps without help, threading five hook and eye fasteners along the front while holding the stiff panels straight, which turns out to feel a lot like buttoning a shirt while being hugged by a bear. By the fifth, I've memorized the lacing order and can direct Veletia's hands instead of just enduring them.

The sixth try is the test. Veletia hands me the garments and retreats to the far side of the room, settling onto a velvet chaise with a cup of tea and an expression of theatrical anticipation.

<<speech "veletia">>
Dress yourself. All four layers. I'll watch.
<</speech>>
<br>

The chemise is simple, over the head, smooth, adjust. Thirty seconds, as promised.

The corset is a nightmare.

Wrapping it around my own midsection without help needs a flexibility the transformation is giving me but hasn't finished delivering. My arms, thinner and more flexible than the prince's ever were, can reach the back panels, but engaging the busk clasps while holding both panels in place turns into an exercise in frustrated contortion. The first clasp catches. The second slips. The third pinches skin through the chemise and I hiss through my teeth while Veletia watches with an expression of serene indifference.

The lacing is worse. Reaching behind myself for the back laces is possible but brutal, my shoulders, narrower now but still carrying muscle from Aldric's training, burning with the effort of the awkward reach. I get the waist to something close to correct, loose enough that Veletia will click her tongue, tight enough that the corset actually holds instead of sagging.

The bodice goes on easier, over the head, arms through the sleeves, settle onto the corset. I manage the back lacing by reaching over my shoulder and pulling the ribbons down, a trick I invent on the spot and am irrationally proud of.

The gown nearly beats me. Stepping into it without someone holding it open means lifting the whole weight of the velvet over my head, and the velvet is heavy, and my arms are tired from the corset fight, and the fabric cascades over my face and tangles in my hair, and I come out through the neck opening looking, I'm fairly sure, less like a queen emerging from her chrysalis and more like a cat losing a fight with a curtain.

But I come out. I hook the back closures, two of three, the top one stays stubbornly out of reach. I shake my hips to settle the skirt. I smooth the front panel. I fix the neckline.

I stand in front of the mirror, dressed, mussed, breathing hard from the effort, hair wild, cheeks flushed, the third back hook still hanging open.

<<speech "veletia">>
Eleven minutes. The back hook's undone. Your hair is a catastrophe. The bodice is twisted a quarter turn. The skirt's pleats are uneven.
<</speech>>
<br>

She sets down her tea.

<<speech "veletia">>
But you're dressed, on your own, in four layers, for the first time in your life.
<</speech>>
<br>

She pauses.

<<speech "veletia">>
I'm unreasonably proud of you, darling.
<</speech>>
<br>

The warmth blooming in my chest has nothing to do with the corset's compression.

[[Session 6 concludes|valetia_session_6_8]]

:: valetia_session_6_8
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>
<<set $valetia_trust += 1>>

Veletia fixes the back hook, corrects the bodice's twist, tames the pleats with quick hands, and spends ten minutes on my hair with a brush and a running monologue about how a queen's hair is a fifth layer most designers ignore at their own risk.

She stands behind me, brushing, and the feeling is disarming. My hair has grown long enough now that the brush sends shivers down my scalp and along the nape of my neck, the fine hairs there standing up as the bristles pass. Each stroke is rhythmic, almost meditative, the sensation traveling down through the scalp, along the spine, settling warm at the base of my skull. I never understood why Maera closes her eyes when someone brushes her hair. I do now.

<<speech "veletia">>
Hair is a frame. The gown is the painting. The bodice is the canvas. The corset is the stretcher. The chemise is the primer, and the hair is the frame that tells the eye where to look first.
<</speech>>
<br>

She draws my hair back from the temples and lets it fall behind my shoulders, the waves catching the light and lying against the midnight velvet like dark rivers on a dark sea.

<<speech "veletia">>
There, complete.
<</speech>>
<br>

I take in the finished result. Four layers, brushed hair, the back hook secured, every seam aligned. The person in the mirror looks assembled. Intentional, like every piece got chosen and placed on purpose, from the whisper of the chemise against skin to the weight of velvet against the floor. Not someone who threw on clothes. Someone built, layer by layer, from the inside out.

<<speech "veletia">>
Here's the lesson, darling. A queen's dress isn't a garment. It's an argument. Every layer supports the one above it. Every piece has a job. Take away the chemise and the corset chafes. Take away the corset and the bodice sags. Take away the bodice and the gown has no shape. A queen who understands her layers understands her power, because power, like a gown, gets built from the ground up.
<</speech>>
<br>

She unclasps a small golden brooch from her own gown, a miniature pair of scissors, her personal mark, and pins it to my bodice at the left shoulder, where the neckline meets the sleeve.

<<speech "veletia">>
A token. For the queen who dressed herself for the first time. Wear it under the gown, where no one sees it. You'll know it's there. That's enough.
<</speech>>
<br>

I touch the little golden scissors with fingers thinner and softer than they used to be, and feel something that isn't quite gratitude and isn't quite affection but lives somewhere near both.

<<speech "mc">>
Thank you, Veletia.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "veletia">>
Valetia, darling. The e goes quiet once you've earned my trust.
<</speech>>
<br>

I didn't know there were two ways to say it. The correction is small, warm, and feels like a key turning in a lock I didn't know existed.

<<speech "veletia">>
Session six is done. Tomorrow, session seven. We teach the gown to walk.
<</speech>>
<br>

She pauses. The theatrical grin comes back.

<<speech "veletia">>
Or really, we teach you to walk in the gown. The gown already knows what it's doing. You, darling, have some catching up to do.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Return to your chamber|valetia_session_6_9]]

:: valetia_session_6_9

I walk back to my chamber in the gown.

Veletia didn't suggest this. She actually suggested I change back into training leathers for the walk. But something, stubbornness, curiosity, the urge to test this new armor against the real world, makes me refuse.

<<speech "mc">>
I'll walk back as I am. The court will see me eventually. Let them see me now.
<</speech>>
<br>

Veletia's eyebrows climb high enough to threaten her hairline. Then she smiles, not the theatrical one, the real one, the one she saves for moments when her student surprises her.

<<speech "veletia">>
Then walk well, darling. Remember, the gown leads. You follow.
<</speech>>
<br>

The corridor between the Bloom Room and my chamber runs maybe two hundred paces. In training leathers I could cover it in under a minute. In the gown it takes nearly five, not because the distance grew but because the gown demands a different kind of walking. Shorter strides. Slower pace. Every step has to account for the skirt's weight, the drape of the pleats, the hem's closeness to the floor. Step too wide and the fabric pulls. Step too fast and the skirt's momentum lags behind the body, bunching at the waist in a way that looks, in the polished steel of a corridor mirror, deeply unflattering.

I learn to walk those two hundred paces the way I learned the feminine guard in the Iron Garden, through repetition, frustration, and the slow surrender of old habits to new requirements. The prince's stride, long, confident, ground eating, is useless here. The gown wants shorter steps, planted firm, each foot placed on purpose. My hips, wider now, provide a natural sway the velvet turns into a gentle, rhythmic swing of the skirt. The corset, rigid, provides stability. My arms, free of weapons for once, hang at my sides, trailing sleeves moving with them like flags in a soft wind.

A servant passes me in the corridor. A young woman, barely older than me, carrying a stack of linens. She stops dead. Her eyes go wide. The linens wobble dangerously.

"Your Highness," she whispers, and before I can answer, she curtseys. Deep, formal, the curtsey of a subject to a queen.

Not to a prince. To a queen.

I incline my head, an instinct that arrives without instruction, the small gracious dip my mother uses with servants, and keep walking. The gown's hem whispers against stone. The trailing sleeves swing. The corset holds me straight, the bodice holds me shaped, the chemise holds me gentle, and I walk through my own castle in four layers of women's clothing, and the corridor doesn't collapse, and the walls don't mock me, and the servant behind me is still curtseying.

I reach my chamber, close the door, and stand with my back against the wood, breathing the belly breath, the Aldric breath, the only breath the corset allows. I press my hands against the velvet over the bodice over the corset over the chemise over my chest, and feel the heartbeat under all four layers, and admit the heart is beating faster than fear alone would explain.

Something else is in there. Not terror, not shame, not the bitter resignation I've worn like a fifth layer since this whole thing started.

It might be excitement.

I don't look at it too closely. Some things grow better in the dark, where scrutiny can't wither them.

[[Rest and prepare for Session 7|valetia_session_6_10]]

:: valetia_session_6_10
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

Maera finds me still in the gown an hour later, standing in front of my chamber mirror, turning slowly, watching the velvet move when my body moves, watching the light catch the nap and shift between blue and black, watching the reflection become someone I don't know but am no longer afraid to meet.

<<speech "maera">>
Well, look at you.
<</speech>>
<br>

I turn. Maera stands in the doorway, arms folded, her small frame silhouetted against the corridor's torchlight. Her dark curls are wild as always. Her eyes are bright with something that flickers between pride and grief too fast for me to tell them apart.

<<speech "maera">>
You look like a proper lady.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
I look like a terrified person in an expensive curtain.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
Aye, and that's what most proper ladies look like underneath. The trick's not showing it.
<</speech>>
<br>

She crosses the room and circles me, exactly the way Veletia did that morning, but with none of the professional distance. Maera's orbit is personal, close, the kind of look someone gives a body they've known since childhood.

<<speech "maera">>
The color suits you. Blue was always your color, even back when you were the most oblivious prince alive and insisted on wearing brown.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
I liked brown.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
Brown didn't like you. Trust me on this.
<</speech>>
<br>

She stops in front of me and reaches up, she has to reach up now, the potions haven't stolen my height but they've shifted my proportions, and in the heeled slippers Veletia gave me I stand half a head above her where we used to be nearly level, and adjusts the brooch at my shoulder. The little golden scissors.

<<speech "maera">>
What's this?
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
A gift. From Veletia.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
A gift, from her.
<</speech>>
<br>

She touches it with one finger, her face softening.

<<speech "maera">>
She likes you. The clothier. She doesn't hand out gifts easily, I've asked.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
You asked?
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
Of course I asked. I ask about everyone who spends time with you. I'm your handmaid. Your protector. If the clothier were plotting your death through whalebone and velvet, I'd want to know ahead of time.
<</speech>>
<br>

I laugh. The sound is lighter than I expect, my voice slipping, like it does more often now, into a register that belongs to the person in the mirror instead of the person in memory.

Maera helps me undress. The gown, then the bodice, then the corset, her fingers finding the laces with the same competence she brings to everything, and finally the chemise, pulled over my head and dropped to the floor, leaving me in undergarments, brassiere, panties, the body visible and changed and a little vulnerable.

She looks at me. I let her. She's seen every stage of this becoming, held my hand through the first examination with Thalara, brought warm cloths for breasts that ached as they grew, pretended not to notice when my voice cracked mid sentence, kept my secrets and my fears and every unvoiced question locked behind a heart that's fierce, sarcastic, and completely loyal.

<<speech "maera">>
You're becoming beautiful, Caelan. I don't mean that as flattery, I mean it as fact. The face, the hair, the body, all of it's turning into something that'll turn heads and break hearts and make the whole court stare. I need you to know the person under the beauty is still the one I've loved since we were kids pulling each other's hair in the nursery.
<</speech>>
<br>

Her voice cracks on the last word. She covers it with a cough and busies herself folding the chemise.

I don't say anything. Some things are too heavy for a response. You just receive them and hold them and carry them, like a crown, as gracefully as you can manage.

I change into my sleeping shift and climb into bed. Maera blows out the candles. At the door she pauses.

<<speech "maera">>
The gown suited you. It truly did.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
Thank you, Maera.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
Tomorrow the clothier's going to make you walk in a skirt. Brace yourself.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
For what?
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
For landing on your backside. Repeatedly, in silk.
<</speech>>
<br>

A grin, wicked and warm, flashes in the dark.

<<speech "maera">>
Sleep well, Your Highness.
<</speech>>
<br>

The door closes. I lie in the dark, the ghost of four layers still pressing against my skin, the golden scissors on the bedside table catching the last light.

Tomorrow, skirts.

I'm not ready. But readiness, I'm learning, is a luxury the crown doesn't offer. The crown only offers forward.

I close my eyes and dream of velvet and mirrors and a woman who isn't finished yet but has stopped being afraid of becoming.

[[Leave Mistress Veletia Roswyn|dressing_bloom]] |<br><br>
[[Leave Royal Dressing Bloom|castle_map_front_select]]

:: valetia_session_7_1
<<set $valetia_session_7 = true>>
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

The Bloom Room has been transformed.

Where yesterday it was an altar of layering, garments hung on forms, mirrors angled for study, today it's a corridor. Veletia has taken over fifty feet of the East Wing's gallery and set it with challenges, chairs placed at intervals for sitting practice, a short flight of wooden steps for stair work, and at the far end a raised platform with a mirror waiting like a judge at the end of a trial.

Three skirts hang from a rack near the entrance, each a different length. A tea length skirt that ends below the knee. An ankle length that brushes the tops of the feet. A full court skirt with a train trailing three feet behind the wearer. Each is paired with a bodice and corset, the same layers from yesterday, the same four part construction. Only the skirt changes.

<<speech "veletia">>
Yesterday we built the queen from the inside out. Today we teach her to move. A queen who can't move in her gown has been swallowed by her wardrobe, and a swallowed queen is a dead queen, because in an emergency she'll trip on her own train and die of embarrassment before the assassin's blade even reaches her throat.
<</speech>>
<br>

She claps her hands. The sound cracks through the gallery like a starting pistol.

<<speech "veletia">>
We start with the tea length. Most forgiving of the three. Think of it as the rapier of skirts, light, quick, full range of motion. The ankle length is the longsword, heavier, more restrictive, more presence. The court train is Frostfang, magnificent, terrifying, and fully capable of killing the woman wielding it if she stops respecting its weight.
<</speech>>
<br>

I look at the three skirts. The weapon comparison feels deliberate, Veletia's gift for translation, turning fabric into a language a warrior already understands.

<<speech "veletia">>
Get dressed. Tea length first. Eight minutes.
<</speech>>
<br>

I dress. Chemise, corset, I manage the busk clasps in under a minute this time, a small victory, bodice, and the tea length skirt. It fastens at the waist with a hook and eye closure, settles onto the hips, and falls to just below my knees. Light and almost weightless after yesterday's velvet, soft heather grey wool that sways gently when I move, the hem brushing my calves with every step.

<<speech "veletia">>
Walk to the end of the gallery and back. I'll watch.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Walk the gallery|valetia_session_7_2]]

:: valetia_session_7_2
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

I walk. The tea length skirt is, as promised, forgiving. The shorter hem doesn't catch at my ankles or tangle in my stride. The light fabric responds to my movement instead of fighting it, swaying with hips wider than memory insists they should be, swinging gently with each step, the hem dancing at my calves.

But walking in a skirt isn't the same as walking in trousers. The absence of fabric between my legs, the open drape of the skirt, air reaching skin the trousers normally cover, is disorienting. Every step carries a whisper of fabric against my inner thighs, a soft sliding friction, intimate and strange at once. My thighs, thicker now with the fat distribution the transformation demands, press together at the top in a way the prince's leaner legs never did, and the skirt catches in the gap and tugs gently with every stride.

<<speech "veletia">>
Shorter steps. You're walking like a soldier on patrol. A queen doesn't patrol, she processes. Shorten your stride by a third. Let the hips do the work.
<</speech>>
<br>

I shorten my stride. The change is immediate, the skirt stops tugging, the fabric settles into a smoother drape, and the hips' natural sway becomes more pronounced. The movement feels different from any walk I've known. Less purposeful. More, the word surfaces before I can stop it, graceful. The body, held upright by the corset, moves from the center. The hips carry the momentum. The feet land with intention instead of haste. The skirt, freed from fighting a soldier's stride, starts to flow.

I reach the end of the gallery, turn, and the turn is the first real lesson.

In trousers, turning is a pivot. Plant, spin, keep going. In a skirt, turning is an arc. The fabric has its own momentum. When my body pivots, the skirt keeps going on its old path for half a second before catching up, wrapping my legs then releasing in a swirl. Turn too sharp and it tangles. Turn too slow and it pools and drags.

The right turn, as Veletia shows me, is a smooth continuous curve, not a pivot but a sweep, the leading foot tracing a crescent on the floor while the skirt follows the body's arc like a wave chasing the shore.

I practice the turn a dozen times. The first four are disasters, skirt tangling, fabric bunching, one attempt Veletia describes, with devastating accuracy, as "a confused maypole." The fifth is passable. The eighth is clean. The twelfth is smooth enough that the hem flares out in a perfect circle before settling back against my calves, and the motion feels not learned but natural, like my body was waiting for permission to move this way.

<<speech "veletia">>
Better work. The tea length is your friend. Now, the ankle length.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[The ankle length skirt|valetia_session_7_3]]

:: valetia_session_7_3
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

The ankle length skirt is the longsword.

Heavier wool, darker grey, falling from the waist in a column that tapers slightly toward the hem and ends just above the tops of the slippers. Denser, more structured, hanging from the hips with a weight that's not just physical but political, a skirt built to be seen. To make a point. To say I'm dressed for the court and the court will attend to me.

I fasten it over the corset and bodice and take my first step.

The difference from the tea length hits immediately and it's humbling. The longer fabric catches at my ankles with every stride, not tangling, but pressing, reminding, constraining. Each step forward needs the leading foot lifted a little higher to clear the hem, which turns the walk into something closer to a processional march. The skirt's weight pulls at my waist, the hook and eye closure straining at the hip, the fabric swinging with a pendulum heaviness that demands respect.

<<speech "veletia">>
The ankle length punishes big strides. It rewards small ones. Smaller steps than the tea length. Plant each foot like you're placing a tile, flat, deliberate, precise. Let the skirt's weight carry the momentum. You're not pushing the skirt, the skirt is pulling you.
<</speech>>
<br>

I adjust. Smaller steps. More deliberate placement. My gait shortens until each stride is maybe eighteen inches, half the soldier's walk, a third of the prince's stride. The corset holds me upright. The hips drive it. The skirt, satisfied with the shorter steps, settles into a smooth, heavy swing that moves with my body instead of against it.

The turn in the ankle length is trickier. The heavier fabric needs a wider arc and more time to complete, and the hem's closeness to the floor means any break in smooth motion risks stepping on my own skirt. I catch myself twice, the second time badly, the hem pulling taut under my right foot and nearly pitching me forward onto my hands.

<<speech "veletia">>
When the skirt catches, don't fight it. Step back, free the fabric, start again. A queen fighting her own gown has already lost the room's respect, and in a court where every stumble gets noticed and filed away by ambitious rivals, one misstep can cost more than a battle.
<</speech>>
<br>

I free the hem. Start again. The turn improves, wider, smoother, my body rotating inside the skirt's circle instead of dragging it along. By the tenth try I can turn in the ankle length without catching the hem, the fabric sweeping around my legs like water around a stone.

But walking and turning are only the start. Veletia positions me in front of the first gallery chair, a high backed wooden seat, the kind found in every council chamber and formal dining hall in Valmoria.

<<speech "veletia">>
Sit down for me.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Learn to sit in a skirt|valetia_session_7_4]]

:: valetia_session_7_4
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

I've been sitting in chairs for twenty years. Council chambers, dining halls, campaign stools, logs in the forest, weapon racks in the Iron Garden. Sitting isn't a skill. Sitting is the absence of standing.

Sitting in a skirt is a whole different discipline.

The prince's method is simple. Approach the chair, turn, drop. The body falls into the seat with the casual authority of someone who never once considered that gravity and fabric might conspire against them. In trousers this works fine. In the ankle length skirt, my first attempt gives Veletia the expression of a woman watching a cart crash in slow motion.

I approach the chair. I turn. I drop.

The skirt, hanging in orderly pleats a moment ago, doesn't drop with me. It bunches. The front panel rides up my thighs on the way down, showing knee and calf and the tops of the stockings underneath. The back panel catches between my body and the seat, pulling taut across my backside in a way that's deeply uncomfortable and, I'm sure, visually absurd. The side panels splay out in uncontrolled wings of grey wool. I end up sitting in the chair looking less like a queen and more like a disgruntled bird trying to roost on a perch too small for it.

<<speech "veletia">>
No, try it again.
<</speech>>
<br>

She demonstrates. The right technique for sitting in a skirt involves four separate motions that look, to an untrained eye, like one fluid movement.

First, approach the chair and stand with the backs of your calves touching the seat's edge. Your body knows the chair is there without looking, a queen never checks behind her before sitting. She trusts the chair. It wouldn't dare not be there.

Second, one hand, the right by tradition, sweeps back in a smooth arc, gathering the back panel of the skirt and pulling it slightly forward. This keeps the fabric from bunching underneath you. The motion should be almost invisible, a casual brush of fingers across the hip, gathering fabric without looking like it.

Third, the descent. Not a drop, a controlled lowering, knees bending, core engaging, and here the corset finally earns its keep, the rigid torso giving the lower body a stable column to descend from without wobbling. The hips lead. The spine stays straight. The lowering is smooth, even, unhurried. The front panel falls naturally over the knees.

Fourth, the arrangement. Once seated, the hands brush the side panels inward, settling the fabric into a smooth drape over the thighs and knees. The ankles cross, right over left or left over right, depending on which way the nearest observer is standing. The spine straightens against the chair back. The hands rest on the lap, on top of the arranged fabric.

I practice twenty three times. The first ten are progressively less catastrophic. By the fifteenth, the back sweep is becoming instinct, my hand reaching behind to clear the fabric before I even think about the descent. By the twentieth, the controlled lowering is smooth enough that the skirt settles into a clean drape instead of a chaotic bundle. By the twenty third, I'm sitting in the gallery chair with the ankle length skirt falling in one unbroken sweep of grey wool from waist to ankle, ankles crossed, hands folded, not a wrinkle or bunch or stray pleat anywhere.

<<speech "veletia">>
Acceptable, now rise.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Rise from the chair|valetia_session_7_5]]

:: valetia_session_7_5
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

Rising from a chair in a skirt is, somehow, harder than sitting down in one.

The prince's method, plant hands on armrests, push, stand. Simple and mechanical. The body's weight shifts upward through arm strength and momentum, the legs straighten, and you're up.

In a skirt, this produces a graceless lurch that yanks the fabric up, shows considerably more leg than dignity allows, and leaves the skirt in a twisted mess that needs thirty seconds of public adjustment to fix.

Veletia's method is an exercise in controlled ascension.

<<speech "veletia">>
A queen rises the way the sun rises, slowly, inevitably, and everyone's watching. The motion starts in the core, not the arms. Feet flat on the floor. Engage the belly, the muscles Aldric so kindly built for you. The spine lifts first. Then the hips. Then the knees straighten. Your hands don't touch the chair. They stay on your lap until you're fully upright, then they fall to your sides. The skirt, if you've arranged it right, falls back into its hang on its own.
<</speech>>
<br>

The first attempt wobbles. The corset, an ally during the seated descent, becomes a hindrance on the way up, it blocks the forward lean my old body used to generate momentum. Without the lean, the rise has to come entirely from core and legs, and the muscles complain with the specific burning objection of tissue being asked to work in an unfamiliar way.

By the sixth attempt, the movement smooths out. The core, built by weeks of the feminine guard, hardened by combat training, kept honest by the corset's constant demand, fires in sequence. Spine straightens. Hips lift. Knees extend. The skirt, gathered properly and undisturbed by flailing arms, falls back into its natural drape like it was never sat on.

<<speech "veletia">>
Again, slower this time. Let the room watch you rise. Let them see the queen come up out of the chair like the chair was beneath her, which, quite literally, it is.
<</speech>>
<br>

I rise slowly. The corset keeps my spine straight. My legs do the work under the skirt, hidden, the effort masked by the fabric's fall. The motion is smooth, unhurried, dignified in a way the prince's arm powered lurch never was.

<<speech "veletia">>
Now you're starting to understand. Sitting and rising aren't functional acts, they're performances. Every moment in the gown is a performance. The court's always watching, and what they see should never be effort. Only ease. Only authority. Only a woman so comfortable in her own clothes that the clothes disappear and all that's left is the queen.
<</speech>>
<br>

The lesson continues with the full court skirt, the Frostfang of skirts, the train trailing behind like the tail of a sleeping dragon. Sitting and rising with a train adds extra choreography, the train has to be swept to one side before sitting, arranged in a graceful arc beside the chair, then gathered in one smooth gesture on the way up so it falls behind the body instead of staying pooled on the floor like a velvet puddle.

[[The curtsey|valetia_session_7_6]]

:: valetia_session_7_6
<<set $femininity_style += 2>>

The curtsey.

I've seen a thousand curtseys in my life. Every noblewoman, every servant, every woman visiting the court performs some version of it, the dip, the nod, the lowering of the body in deference to the crown. I've received curtseys since birth, nodded graciously in return, never once thought about the mechanics of it any more than I've thought about breathing.

Now I'm on the other side of the gesture, and it's a lot more complicated than breathing.

<<speech "veletia">>
The curtsey is a language spoken with the body. A shallow curtsey says I see you. A medium curtsey says I respect you. A deep curtsey says I yield to you. The full court curtsey, the deep bow before the crown, says my body, my will, and my loyalty are yours. A queen has to know all four, because a queen both gives curtseys and receives them, to her own mother, to visiting monarchs, to the gods at temple.
<</speech>>
<br>

She shows me the basic court curtsey. The mechanics go like this.

Right foot slides behind the left, crossing at the ankle. Weight shifts to the back foot. Both knees bend, not just the back one, a variation Veletia dismisses as "the barbarian squat." Both bend together, lowering the body straight down instead of tipping it forward. The spine stays vertical, the corset enforces this whether you like it or not. The head dips slightly forward. The hands gather the sides of the skirt, lifting the fabric just enough to keep from stepping on the hem during the descent.

How deep the curtsey goes depends on how far the knees bend. Shallow, maybe four inches of descent, barely a dip. Medium, eight to ten inches, a clear lowering. Deep, full knee bend, nearly to the floor, the back knee hovering just above it.

I try the shallow curtsey first. Straightforward, slide, shift, dip, rise. The corset keeps me aligned. The skirt's weight pulls down during the descent and I have to counter it with my core to keep the dip from turning into a collapse. But the motion is manageable, almost intuitive, and by the third try I produce a shallow curtsey Veletia acknowledges with a nod.

The medium curtsey asks more of the legs. The deeper bend needs quad strength, the same muscles that power the drop step in combat, and Aldric's training surfaces in a context I never expected. The feminine guard's focus on hip driven movement, on core stability, on lowering the body's center of gravity under control, all of it applies here. The curtsey, I realize with a small jolt, is the social version of the drop step. Same muscles. Same balance. Same controlled descent and rise.

The body that can drop under a sword swing can curtsey before a queen. The warrior's training and the queen's training are the same training, spoken in different languages.

<<speech "veletia">>
The deep curtsey now. This is the one you'll do for your mother at formal occasions. It's an act of submission, not weakness, acknowledgment. You lower yourself to recognize the crown's authority, and in doing it you prove you have the strength to lower yourself and rise again.
<</speech>>
<br>

The deep curtsey is the full commitment. I slide my right foot back, shift my weight, and lower, down, down, knees bending to their limit, the skirt pooling around me in a circle of grey wool, the spine held straight by the corset, my head dipping until my gaze meets the floor. At the bottom my back knee hovers an inch above the ground. My thighs burn. My core fights to hold the vertical spine. The corset keeps me rigid through what's basically a very deep squat in formal clothing.

I hold the position for a three count, the customary pause before the sovereign acknowledges the curtsey and the curtsier rises.

Then I rise. Slowly and smoothly, legs pressing up, core stabilizing, spine straight, the skirt falling back into its natural drape as my body climbs. The motion is clean and controlled, and when I'm back at full height and meeting my own eyes in the gallery mirror, it feels surprisingly powerful. Lowering yourself and rising again on your own power reads less like submission and more like a demonstration. I can go this low, the curtsey says. I can come back from it too, on my own terms. Watch me do it.

<<speech "veletia">>
Magnificent work. Now, with the full court train.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[The court curtsey with train|valetia_session_7_7]]

:: valetia_session_7_7
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

The court skirt's train adds a whole new layer of complexity to the curtsey, one that borders on architectural. The three feet of fabric trailing behind me have to be managed through the descent, swept to one side with a casual hand, then allowed to pool gracefully around my body at the bottom of the curtsey, framing me like petals around the center of a flower.

The first attempt with the train is a tangle. It catches under my back foot mid descent and yanks the waist closure hard enough to pop a hook and eye. The skirt sags. The curtsey collapses. I end up in an undignified heap of wool and embarrassment, one hand clutching the gaping waistband, the other flat on the floor for balance.

<<speech "veletia">>
The train isn't your enemy. It's your cloak. Sweep it, don't fight it. One motion of the right hand, behind the body, as you start the descent, the train follows the hand and settles beside you. Think of it like Aldric's cape. Has he ever once tangled in his own cloak?
<</speech>>
<br>

She has a point. Aldric's half cloak moves with him like part of his own body, never snagging, never catching, always falling exactly where it should. Not because the cloak is magic, because he's worn it so long his body accounts for its weight without thinking.

The train works the same way. A weight I have to learn to fold into the way I move, to feel as an extension instead of a burden.

The second attempt goes better. I sweep the train as I descend, and it settles in a graceful arc to my right. The curtsey goes deep, knees bending, spine held straight by the corset, and at the bottom I'm surrounded by a fan of fabric spread in a semicircle.

<<speech "veletia">>
Better this time. The sweep was a touch late, practice until it lands with the first knee bend. On the way up, your left hand gathers the train behind you as you rise, so it falls naturally into its trailing position. Skip that and you'll step on it and renew your acquaintance with the floor.
<</speech>>
<br>

I practice the court curtsey with train fourteen times. By the seventh, the sweep lands with the descent. By the tenth, the gather on the rise is smooth. By the fourteenth, the whole sequence, approach, sweep, descend, hold, rise, gather, walk, flows as one continuous movement without a single snag.

Veletia applauds. A genuine, delighted round of it, her ring heavy hands clapping with the enthusiasm of a woman watching her creation succeed.

<<speech "veletia">>
The curtsey is mastered. Now, the final challenge. The stairs.
<</speech>>
<br>

She points toward the wooden steps at the far end of the gallery. Three up, a platform, three down.

<<speech "veletia">>
A queen climbs and descends stairs before the court more often than she ever swings a sword. Every entrance to the Great Hall, every approach to the throne, every exit from every room in this castle needs stairs. If you can fight a goblin in a ravine, you can manage three steps in a skirt. Same principle. Know where your feet are, know where the fabric is, and never, ever look down.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[Climb the stairs|valetia_session_7_8]]

:: valetia_session_7_8
<<set $femininity_style += 1>>

Climbing stairs in the ankle length skirt needs a technique I never saw coming, the lift and step.

In trousers, climbing stairs is automatic, foot up, plant, push, next foot. The fabric moves with the legs without a fight. In the ankle length skirt, the hem catches at the ankle of the climbing foot, pulling the fabric tight across my knee and threatening to pitch me face first into the steps.

<<speech "veletia">>
Left hand gathers the front panel of the skirt, just the front, not the sides, and lifts it to ankle height. No higher. Lifting the skirt past the ankle is only for puddles or mud, and even then you'll catch a disapproving look from Lady Serelyne. Right hand rests on the banister, or hangs at your side if there's no banister. The body climbs with the spine straight, the corset will see to that, and each step lands flat, not toe first. Flat landing keeps your heel from catching the hem behind you.
<</speech>>
<br>

I gather the front panel. The fabric bunches in my left hand, smooth, warm, familiar from hours of handling. I lift it to ankle height, revealing the slippers underneath, and climb.

The first step is cautious. The second, steadier. The third puts me on the platform without a problem, the skirt released from my hand, falling back to full length as I straighten up.

<<speech "veletia">>
Good, now go down.
<</speech>>
<br>

Descending is harder. The instinct is to look down, to watch my feet, to check the hem isn't underfoot. But Veletia's instruction is absolute. A queen doesn't look down. She looks forward. She trusts her feet to find the steps. She trusts the skirt to follow. She trusts the whole architecture of her body and her clothing to carry her safely from height to floor.

I descend. Left hand gathers the front panel again. Right hand finds the banister, a concession to practicality Veletia allows for training but expects me to drop within a week. Each step lands flat, heel first, then the toe, weight shifting forward and down with the same controlled precision as a combat descent.

The hem grazes the edges of the steps as I go down, fabric brushing the wood with a whisper that sounds, in the gallery's quiet, like pages turning. The sound is oddly pleasant, a soft textile murmur keeping time with each step like a quiet heartbeat.

I reach the bottom. Turn, the smooth sweeping turn from earlier. Face the mirror at the far end of the gallery.

In it, a woman in grey wool descends invisible stairs, spine straight, one hand holding the fabric with easy grace, face forward, eyes ahead. She doesn't look down. She trusts.

<<speech "veletia">>
Now, the court skirt, with train.
<</speech>>
<br>

The court skirt and its three foot train turn the stair challenge into a military operation. Climbing needs the left hand gathering the front panel while the right reaches back to lift the train clear of the steps, two motions at once, while climbing, while keeping the spine straight, while not looking down.

Going down is worse. The train, trailing behind, has to be managed so it drapes down the steps behind me instead of pooling on the step I'm standing on, pooling means stepping on the train, which means falling, which means dying of embarrassment in front of the whole court, which Veletia informs me actually happened to a duchess in the southern kingdoms two centuries ago, though she had the good grace to only break her wrist.

I practice the court skirted stair climb and descent twenty one times. I catch the train twice. I stumble once. By the fifteenth repetition, managing the front panel and the train with each hand separately starts to feel automatic, the same kind of muscle memory the dual blade drills demanded, left hand and right hand doing different jobs at once, like they belong to two people sharing a body.

By the twenty first, I go down the three wooden steps in the full court skirt smoothly enough that Veletia stops mid sip of her tea and watches me reach the bottom with the look of a woman who's just seen her finest dress walk off the mannequin on its own.

<<speech "veletia">>
You're ready for the final assessment.
<</speech>>
<br>

[[The final assessment|valetia_session_7_9]]

:: valetia_session_7_9
<<set $femininity_style += 2>>

The final assessment is the full sequence. Walk the gallery in the ankle length formal skirt, sit in the chair, rise, curtsey to an imagined sovereign, climb the stairs, turn on the platform, descend the stairs, walk to the mirror at the end. One continuous performance. No stops. No corrections. No second chances.

Veletia stands at the gallery's midpoint, arms crossed, her face set into the serious mask she wears when she's being Professional instead of Theatrical. Beside her, uninvited and unexpected, stands a second observer.

Maera, arms folded, watching.

My handmaid has slipped into the gallery sometime during the stair practice, moving with the quiet efficiency that makes her invaluable as a confidante and a little unsettling as a spy. She leans against the wall, arms folded, dark curls falling across her forehead, her face unreadable.

I meet her eyes. She gives a single, almost invisible nod. The nod of a woman who's known me since childhood and came to witness something she understands matters, even if she couldn't say exactly why.

I stand at the gallery entrance. Chemise, corset, bodice, ankle length skirt. Four layers. The golden scissors pinned under the bodice, hidden, pressing against the corset over my heart. Hair brushed, falling past my shoulders. Soft leather slippers with a one inch heel, enough to shift my posture without wrecking my balance.

I walk.

The stride is short, deliberate, each foot placed with the precision Veletia has drilled into me over hours of practice. The skirt sways. The hem brushes the tops of the slippers. The corset holds me straight. My hips carry the momentum, the natural sway the transformation gave me, or inflicted on me, depending on the moment's mood, amplified by the skirt's weight into a gentle, rhythmic swing.

I reach the chair. Stand with my calves touching the seat. Right hand sweeps behind to clear the back panel. The descent is smooth, knees bending, core engaged, spine vertical, the lowering controlled and unhurried. The skirt falls into a clean drape over my knees. Ankles crossed. Hands folded.

A breath, then another.

I rise. Core first. Spine lifting. Hips ascending. Knees straightening. The skirt falling back into its natural hang without a single wrinkle out of place. The motion is smooth enough that the fabric barely moves, my body rising inside the skirt like a tide rising inside a harbor, lifting everything with it.

The curtsey. I choose the deep court curtsey, full descent, the back knee hovering above the floor, the spine held straight by whalebone and will. Down, down, the skirt pooling around me, my head inclined, my body at its lowest point. I hold for three heartbeats.

I rise, slowly and inevitably, the way the sun rises.

The stairs. Left hand gathers the front panel. Right hand at my side, no banister, as Veletia expects. Three steps up, each one flat planted, the skirt managed, the fabric lifting and falling with every climb. The platform. The turn, smooth, sweeping, the skirt arcing around my legs in a clean curve.

Three steps down. The descent is steady, controlled, spine straight, eyes forward. Not looking down. Trusting my feet. Trusting the fabric. Trusting my body.

I reach the bottom. Walk the last twenty paces to the mirror. I stop there.

The gallery goes quiet. Veletia says nothing. Maera says nothing. The only sound is the whisper of the hem against the marble and distant birdsong drifting in from the East Wing windows.

I look at the mirror. The woman looking back has walked a gallery, sat in a chair, risen like the sun, curtseyed to a queen, climbed and descended stairs, and never once, not once, stumbled, caught, tangled, lurched, or let the effort show through the elegance.

She looks like someone who belongs in the gown. Who has always belonged in it. Who was just waiting for the body and the clothing and the training to finally line up into something that feels, at last, like home.

[[Session 7 concludes|valetia_session_7_10]]

:: valetia_session_7_10
<<set $femininity_style += 2>>
<<set $valetia_trust += 2>>

Veletia breaks the silence first. She uncrosses her arms, and her hands come together in a slow, measured clap that echoes through the gallery like a heartbeat.

<<speech "veletia">>
Session seven, complete. Your walk is clean. Your sitting is correct. Your rising is elegant. Your curtsey wouldn't embarrass you in front of any court on this continent. Your stairs are solid and they'll get better with practice.
<</speech>>
<br>

She pauses. Her green eyes are bright.

<<speech "veletia">>
You're becoming a woman who moves like she's never moved any other way, and that, darling, is the highest compliment I know how to give.
<</speech>>
<br>

She crosses the gallery and takes both my hands in hers. Her fingers are warm, calloused at the tips from needlework, cold rings pressing against my knuckles.

<<speech "veletia">>
Seven sessions. We started with a prince who didn't know how to clasp a brassiere, and here we are, a woman in four layers who can walk a gallery, sit a chair, and curtsey before a queen without a single misstep. What's left builds on this, color, jewelry, cosmetics, hair, dance, the advanced arts. But the foundation's built, and it's strong.
<</speech>>
<br>

She releases my hands, steps back, and drops into a curtsey of her own, deep, perfect, theatrical, her silk gown pooling around her in a ring of color. When she rises, her eyes are suspiciously bright.

<<speech "veletia">>
I curtsey to my student. My queen in progress. My masterwork.
<</speech>>
<br>

Maera, who's watched the whole assessment without a word, pushes off the wall and walks over. She looks up at me, her small frame dwarfed by my height in the heeled slippers, and her dark eyes hold that same complicated mix of pride and grief I caught the night before.

<<speech "maera">>
You didn't fall on your backside. I owe Veletia a coin.
<</speech>>
<br>

I laugh. The sound is lighter still than yesterday, my voice continuing its slow drift toward a register that belongs to the woman in the mirror instead of the prince in memory. The laugh feels good. Clean and untangled from bitterness.

<<speech "maera">>
Caelan, truly, that was
<</speech>>
<br>

She stops and swallows hard. Her sarcasm, usually as solid as Aldric's silence, cracks just enough to show the softness underneath.

<<speech "maera">>
That's something I won't forget.
<</speech>>
<br>

She reaches up and straightens my bodice at the shoulder, a small maternal gesture, the kind Maera makes without thinking, one of a thousand tiny adjustments she's been making to me since we were children and she decided, unilaterally and without asking, that I was hers to look after.

<<speech "maera">>
Your mother should see you. In the gown. Walking like that.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "mc">>
Not yet. Not until I can do it without thinking about it.
<</speech>>
<br>

<<speech "maera">>
Then practice. Because the court won't be thinking about anything else.
<</speech>>
<br>

She turns and leaves the gallery, and as she passes Veletia the two women trade a look that's brief and wordless and full of the particular understanding shared by two people who care about the same person from different angles.

I stand alone in the gallery. Afternoon sun paints long gold rectangles across the marble floor. The skirt hangs from my hips in its clean, obedient drape. The corset holds me. The bodice frames me. The chemise whispers against my skin.

Four layers. Four purposes. Four steps between nakedness and sovereignty.

I curtsey to the empty gallery, a practice curtsey, unnecessary and a little self indulgent, performed for no audience but the mirror and the dust and the lingering smell of lavender and cedar. The descent is smooth. The rise is strong.

Tomorrow, Aldric's training yard. The rapier. The dagger. The feminine guard. My body moving through combat with the same core strength that just carried me through a gallery in a gown.

Two trainings. Two languages. One body learning to speak both.

I turn from the mirror and walk back toward my chamber, the skirt's hem whispering against the floor, heels clicking softly on marble, hair swaying against velvet covered shoulders. A servant passes me in the corridor and curtseys. A guard salutes. Neither hesitates. Neither questions. They see a woman in formal clothing moving through the castle with purpose, and they respond the way you'd expect.

The prince would have gone unnoticed in training leathers. The queen in progress is visible in every thread, seen now in a way that's impossible to look past.

Forward is the only direction left.

I let that settle for a moment right there in the corridor. Then I keep walking.

[[Return to the castle|castle_map_front_select]]
